---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import StarsSim from '../../simulators/stars/StarsSim.astro';
import { starsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/stars';

const toc = [
  { id: 'birth', label: 'How stars are born' },
  { id: 'main', label: 'The main sequence' },
  { id: 'hr', label: 'Reading the HR diagram' },
  { id: 'low', label: 'Low- and medium-mass stars' },
  { id: 'massive', label: 'Massive stars' },
  { id: 'remnants', label: 'Stellar remnants' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'The numbers behind the model' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Chapter 18.4: The H–R Diagram. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/18-4-the-h-r-diagram' },
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Chapter 22: Stars from Adolescence to Old Age. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/22-introduction' },
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Chapter 23: The Death of Stars. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/23-introduction' },
  { text: 'Kippenhahn, R., Weigert, A., & Weiss, A. (2012). Stellar Structure and Evolution (2nd ed.). Springer.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="astronomy"
  topic="stellar-life-cycle"
  description="Interactive stellar life cycle simulator: choose a star’s mass and follow it across the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram from protostar to white dwarf, neutron star or black hole."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={starsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Stars are born, shine for millions or billions of years, and die, some quietly and some in explosions visible across
      the universe. The one thing that decides a star’s story is the mass it starts with. Pick a mass and watch its life
      unfold on the astronomer’s favourite chart, the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <StarsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>starting mass</strong> with the slider or a preset, then press <strong>Start</strong>.</li>
      <li>Watch the star’s dot move across the <strong>HR diagram</strong>; the dashed line shows where it will go next.</li>
      <li>On the right, see the star’s size and colour change, with the Sun’s size for comparison.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Skip to next stage</strong> to jump ahead, and read what is happening in the panel.</li>
      <li>Compare the <strong>Time on the main sequence</strong> and <strong>How it ends</strong> readouts for small and large stars.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="birth">How stars are born</h2>
  <p>
    Stars form inside cold, dense clouds of gas and dust called <strong>nebulae</strong>. When part of a cloud becomes
    dense enough, gravity makes it collapse. The collapsing clump heats up and glows as a <strong>protostar</strong>,
    often surrounded by a spinning disc of material from which planets may later form. When the core reaches about 10
    million °C, hydrogen fusion begins and a true star is born.
  </p>

  <h2 id="main">The main sequence</h2>
  <p>
    For about 90 % of its life, a star fuses hydrogen into helium in its core. The energy released pushes outward and
    balances the inward pull of gravity, so the star is stable. This long, steady phase is the <strong>main
    sequence</strong>. Massive stars are much hotter, brighter and bluer, but they use up their fuel far faster: a star of
    30 Suns lasts only a few million years, while the Sun lasts about 10 billion and a small red dwarf can last trillions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hr">Reading the HR diagram</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Hertzsprung–Russell (HR) diagram</strong> plots a star’s luminosity (how much light it gives out) against
    its surface temperature. By tradition, temperature increases to the <strong>left</strong>. Most stars fall on a
    diagonal band, the main sequence. <strong>Giants and supergiants</strong> sit at the top right, cool but huge.
    <strong>White dwarfs</strong> sit at the bottom left, hot but tiny. As a star ages it moves across the diagram, so
    each position tells you about its stage of life.
  </p>

  <h2 id="low">Low- and medium-mass stars</h2>
  <p>
    When a star like the Sun runs out of hydrogen in its core, the core shrinks and heats while the outer layers swell
    into a <strong>red giant</strong>, possibly a hundred times wider than before. Helium then fuses into carbon and
    oxygen. Eventually the star gently puffs off its outer layers, forming a glowing <strong>planetary nebula</strong>
    (nothing to do with planets; early telescopes made them look like discs). The exposed core remains as a
    <strong>white dwarf</strong>. Red dwarfs, the most common stars of all, are expected to fade into white dwarfs
    without ever becoming giants, but they live so long that none has done so yet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="massive">Massive stars</h2>
  <p>
    Stars above about 8 solar masses become <strong>red supergiants</strong> and fuse ever-heavier elements in shells:
    carbon, neon, oxygen, silicon and finally iron. Fusing iron absorbs energy instead of releasing it, so once the core
    is iron it collapses in less than a second. The rebound and a flood of neutrinos blow the star apart in a
    <strong>core-collapse supernova</strong>, which can briefly outshine an entire galaxy and scatters newly made elements
    into space. The atoms in your blood and bones were made in stars like these.
  </p>

  <h2 id="remnants">Stellar remnants</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>White dwarf:</strong> about Earth-sized, with roughly the Sun’s mass. A teaspoon weighs several tonnes.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutron star:</strong> about 20 km across, packing more than the Sun’s mass. Some spin rapidly and sweep beams of radio waves across space as <strong>pulsars</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Black hole:</strong> formed when the collapsing core is too massive (very roughly, from stars above 20–25 Suns) for anything to stop the collapse.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="formulas">The numbers behind the model</h2>
  <p>The simulator uses standard approximate relations for main-sequence stars (in units of the Sun):</p>
  <p><code class="formula">L ≈ M³·⁵ · R ≈ M⁰·⁵⁷ to M⁰·⁸ · L = 4πR²σT⁴ · lifetime ≈ 10 billion years × M⁻²·⁵</code></p>
  <p>
    The last relation explains the key idea: doubling a star’s mass gives it twice the fuel but makes it about 11 times
    brighter, so it lives less than a fifth as long.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Luminosity</dt>
    <dd>The total power a star gives out as light, often given relative to the Sun.</dd>
    <dt>Solar mass (M☉)</dt>
    <dd>The mass of the Sun, about 2 × 10³⁰ kg, used as a unit for stars.</dd>
    <dt>Supernova</dt>
    <dd>The explosive death of a massive star (or of a white dwarf in a binary system).</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The Sun will explode as a supernova.”</strong> It is far too small; it will end as a white dwarf.</li>
    <li><strong>“Black holes suck everything in.”</strong> From far away, a black hole’s gravity is the same as any object of the same mass.</li>
    <li><strong>“Stars burn like fire.”</strong> Their energy comes from nuclear fusion, not chemical burning.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A star’s starting mass determines its brightness, lifetime and fate.</li>
    <li>Massive stars live fast and die young in supernovae; small stars live for billions to trillions of years.</li>
    <li>Sun-like stars end as white dwarfs; massive stars leave neutron stars or black holes.</li>
    <li>The HR diagram maps a star’s temperature and luminosity, and so its life stage.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: tracks after the main sequence are schematic, showing typical positions for each stage rather than a detailed stellar-evolution calculation. Mass boundaries such as 8 and 20 solar masses are approximate.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
